<section class="section section--tint" id="about" aria-labelledby="about-title">
  <div class="wrap split">
    <div>
      <h2 id="about-title">About</h2>
      <p class="section__lead">Trained in accounting. In the habit of building.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="split__body">
      <p>I'm a CPA and a Certified ScrumMaster, based in Sydney. I studied accounting at UNSW, and somewhere along the way I started writing code to remove the repetitive parts of the work. That turned into building AI workflows, and then into building software for its own sake.</p>
      <p>The two halves keep informing each other. Accounting taught me that a number is only as good as the evidence under it and the person prepared to sign it. Software taught me that anything done twice by hand should be written down precisely enough for a machine to do it the third time. Most of what I write here sits where those two ideas meet.</p>
      <h3 class="split__subhead">How I work</h3>
      <ul class="principles">
        <li><strong>The number needs an owner.</strong> A model can prepare it. A person answers for it.</li>
        <li><strong>Evidence first.</strong> If I can't show where a figure came from, I don't have a figure.</li>
        <li><strong>Deterministic where it matters.</strong> Rules and arithmetic belong in code, not in a prompt.</li>
        <li><strong>Small permissions.</strong> Give a tool the least access that lets it do the job.</li>
        <li><strong>Write it down.</strong> A procedure that lives in one head cannot be checked or handed over.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

